 495 Nov 11 14:52 slope_interval_vs_decision_rule.png.prov.json\n53-\\begin{figure}[t]\n54-\\centering\n55:\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n56-\\caption{Overview of the width-dependence test. Delay is the gap between the first checkpoint at which training accuracy reaches $0.99$ and the first at which held-out accuracy does, floored at one probe interval and set to the step limit if either threshold is never reached. The slope of $\\ln$(delay) against $\\log_2$(width) is estimated over five widths and four seeds, with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval; the interval, not the point estimate, determines the verdict. In this study the interval straddles zero and the verdict is inconclusive.}\n57-\\label{fig:overview}\n58-\\end{figure}\n59-\n60-\\section{Related Work}\n61-\\label{sec:related}\n62-\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} analyze small transformers trained on modular addition mechanistically and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. Empirical studies have varied some of those knobs on modular arithmetic or related algorithmic tasks: \\citet{Doshi2023ToGO} study modular arithmetic with corrupted labels and examine how generalization and memorization coexist under regularization; \\citet{Thilak2022TheSM} associate grokking under adaptive optimizers with cyclic behavior of the last-layer norm; and \\citet{Prieto2025GrokkingAT} attribute the absence of grokking without regularization to numerical instability in the softmax. We do not re-derive these findings, and none of them has, as far as their stated scope and our non-exhaustive reading indicate, measured delay against width under a fixed protocol, which is the quantity examined here; the present study does not test the accounts against each other.\n63-\n--\n149-\\begin{figure}[t]\n150-\\centering\n151:\\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/slope_interval_vs_decision_rule.pdf}\n152-\\caption{The estimated slope and its 95\\% interval against the three decision regions. The interval straddles zero, so the verdict is inconclusive. The single-seed reference slope lies inside the interval. Dotted lines mark the largest slope magnitude attainable ($1.107$) when every width-wise mean delay is confined to $[250, 10{,}000]$.}\n153-\\label{fig:slope}\n154-\\end{figure}\n155-\n156-\\subsection{Resolution of the design}\n157-\\label{sec:resolution}\n158-An inconclusive verdict is informative only relative to the effects the design could have resolved. Three properties of the protocol, all computed from its constants and the reported interval (Table~\\ref{tab:resolution}), limit that resolution.\n159-% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json#full]]\n--\n353-\\begin{figure}[h]\n354-\\centering\n355:\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variants_descriptive.pdf}\n356-\\caption{Development-plane comparison of three configurations (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, two seeds; not the sealed evaluation). (a) Per-seed and mean held-out-accuracy AUC. (b) Number of the six runs censored at the step limit.}\n357-\\label{fig:dev}\n358-\\end{figure}\n359-\n360-\\begin{table}[h]\n361-\\centering\n362-\\caption{Development-plane summary (two seeds $\\times$ three widths $=6$ runs per configuration).}\n363-\\label{tab:si-dev}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02e09a93-f225-4f12-87d4-eb1573f68698","uuid":"add6f864-6201-4a17-8fed-0da91a2d64c3","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:58:32.279Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        6\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"protocol_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 9\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 40,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/protocol_overview.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/protocol_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": false,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        2\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"slope_interval_vs_decision_rule\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 8.5,\n        \"ticks\": 8.5,\n        \"title\": 9\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 0.8,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 42,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/slope_interval_vs_decision_rule.pdf\",\